      "skills/method/references/upstream.md": "# Upstream provenance and bounded adaptation\n\n- Repository: https://github.com/mohitagw15856/pm-claude-skills\n- Pinned commit: 04fb3213980dbb711e422757efac509b26438358\n- Exact source path: skills/climate-risk-assessment/SKILL.md\n- Pinned source URL: https://github.com/mohitagw15856/pm-claude-skills/blob/04fb3213980dbb711e422757efac509b26438358/skills/climate-risk-assessment/SKILL.md\n- License: MIT; the complete notice is saved in upstream-license.txt.\n- Primary method cross-check: IPCC AR6 Working Group II, Climate Change 2022: Impacts, Adaptation and Vulnerability, https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/wg2/\n\nBounded concepts adapted: separate physical and transition risk; frame risk through hazard, exposure and vulnerability; state scenario and horizon; report ranges, confidence and gaps; structure adaptation options; record monitoring triggers.\n\nExplicitly excluded: upstream default years, scenario labels, assumed locations, sample risk scores, invented revenue/financial ranges, automatic external data acquisition, current TCFD/ESRS/ISSB compliance claims, capital allocation, and engineering, actuarial, insurance, legal or emergency conclusions. Examples are not factual defaults. The local Skill and assets own the OpenCorvus execution contract.\n",
      "skills/method/SKILL.md": "---\nname: climate-risk-adaptation-method\ndescription: Build scenario-explicit climate hazard, exposure, vulnerability, consequence, and adaptation-pathway evidence packs. Use for physical or transition climate-risk framing, scenario and horizon comparison, cascading dependency review, adaptation options, monitoring triggers, residual risk, and maladaptation analysis without issuing engineering, actuarial, legal, disclosure, emergency, or capital decisions.\n---\n\n# Climate Risk Adaptation Method\n\n## Freeze the basis\n\n1. Record system/asset/population boundary, geography, present-day baseline, hazard sources, scenarios/horizons, models/ensembles/downscaling, units, decision question, equity/community limits and qualified owners.\n2. Assign stable IDs to hazards, sources, scenarios, horizons, exposed objects/groups, vulnerabilities, dependencies, consequences, options, pathways, triggers and decisions.\n3. Run hazard/scenario, exposure/vulnerability/consequence, and option/pathway work independently. Join only complete source-addressable results.\n\n## Apply the method\n\n- Keep physical and transition risk, and acute and chronic hazards, distinct.\n- Link every risk through explicit hazard, exposure and vulnerability evidence. Preserve baseline, model, ensemble, scenario, horizon, spatial support, units and source date.\n- Report ranges, member counts, confidence basis, model/data/structural uncertainty and gaps. Do not average incompatible scenarios or treat model spread as total uncertainty.\n- Estimate physical or financial consequence only with a supplied validated vulnerability/damage function and compatible units; otherwise keep consequence qualitative and evidence-linked.\n- Trace direct, indirect and cascading dependencies without double counting. Record differential exposure, adaptive capacity, privacy, distributional effects and community evidence.\n- Structure options by prerequisites, lead time, life, reversibility, performance evidence, triggers, co-benefits, maladaptation and residual risk. Build conditional pathways; do not invent scores, probabilities, costs, rankings or default scenarios.\n\n## Produce evidence\n\nUse all five assets: hazard-source register, exposure/vulnerability/dependency ledger, scenario matrix, option/pathway/trigger register, and decision pack. Every material row carries ID, unit, source/version/date, owner/reviewer, applicability, uncertainty, assumptions and decision status.\n\nStop on incompatible baselines, missing scenario/model metadata, unjustified downscaling, unvalidated consequence functions, sensitive locations, missing community authority or professional-design questions.\n\n## Review discipline\n\n- Preserve observed climate, model output, scenario assumption, exposure fact, vulnerability function, attributed stakeholder statement and analyst inference as different evidence kinds.\n- A scenario is not a forecast probability. Never combine scenario outputs into an expected value unless an authorized decision method provides probabilities and supports that use.\n- Match hazard support to exposure geography and decision scale. Record coarse-resolution, bias-adjustment, model dependence and local-data limitations beside every derived result.\n- Separate direct, indirect and cascading consequences; name dependency direction and apply a declared double-counting rule.\n- Evaluate each adaptation option across scenarios for prerequisites, lead time, useful life, reversibility, trigger observability, residual risk, maladaptation and distributional effect.\n- Record who bears costs, receives benefits and controls the decision. Missing affected-community evidence remains a gap, not assumed consent.\n- At the join, show robust enabling actions and scenario-contingent pathways without converting evidence into a professional recommendation or capital ranking.\n\n## Authority boundary\n\nDo not issue engineering, actuarial, insurance, legal, disclosure, emergency, land-use, relocation, procurement, investment or capital decisions. Require qualified climate, engineering, actuarial, emergency, regulatory, finance, public/community and asset-owner review.\n\nRead [upstream provenance](references/upstream.md). Apply only its bounded concepts; the local Skill is the runtime source of truth.\n",
